WEBVTT

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:05.693
At 8:59 on Monday, a meeting disappeared
from Daniel's calendar.

00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:08.742
Nobody told him why.

00:00:09.322 --> 00:00:12.984
And by lunchtime, he had decided exactly
what it meant.

00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:22.095
A week earlier, Ruth had made her
decision.

00:00:22.575 --> 00:00:24.254
Leah got the promotion.

00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:29.595
<v Daniel>Senior Analyst Carter. Congratulations.
You earned it.

00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:32.080
<v Leah>You're really not annoyed?

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.673
<v Daniel>I'd have been annoyed if it was anyone
else.

00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:40.397
<v Graham>Congratulations, Leah. Ostrel's all yours
now.

00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:44.820
<v Graham>Don't worry about the meeting list. I'll
sort it.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.781
He meant it.

00:00:48.261 --> 00:00:50.906
Which is what made the next week so
strange.

00:00:52.556 --> 00:00:56.955
At ten o'clock, three people walked into
the Ostrel meeting: Graham, Ruth,

00:00:56.975 --> 00:00:57.975
and Leah.

00:00:58.093 --> 00:01:00.134
Daniel watched them through the glass,

00:01:00.154 --> 00:01:02.106
and pretended to read a spreadsheet.

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:06.172
<v Leah>Morning! Sorry, running to Ruth.

00:01:07.672 --> 00:01:10.837
She was busy. Daniel heard: she can't even
look at me.

00:01:12.807 --> 00:01:16.997
So Daniel sent a careful little message.
Eighteen minutes later,

00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:18.017
Graham replied.

00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:20.738
One word. And a full stop.

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:28.724
By midnight, Daniel had written a whole
story.

00:01:29.204 --> 00:01:33.002
Graham was punishing him for correcting
the record. Twice.

00:01:33.482 --> 00:01:36.387
Leah had asked for him to be taken off the
account,

00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:37.898
now that she was the boss.

00:01:38.378 --> 00:01:41.912
And everyone in that meeting had looked at
his empty chair,

00:01:41.932 --> 00:01:42.932
and understood.

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:50.893
At fourteen minutes past midnight, he
wrote a reply. Then, thankfully,

00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:52.020
he didn't send it.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:57.746
Here's what he actually knew. His name
came off an invite.

00:01:57.766 --> 00:02:00.953
Graham wrote one word. Leah leads the
account now.

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.880
Everything on the right was a story he'd
built on top.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.139
Some of it might even have been true.

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:11.418
Why does a missing calendar invite feel
like a punch?

00:02:11.438 --> 00:02:14.972
Psychologists have spent decades on
exactly that question.

00:02:15.842 --> 00:02:17.597
In one well-known experiment,

00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:21.474
people played a simple online game of
catch with two strangers.

00:02:21.954 --> 00:02:25.602
After a few throws, the others quietly
stopped passing them the ball.

00:02:26.182 --> 00:02:30.078
Being left out made people feel worse, and
less like they belonged,

00:02:30.098 --> 00:02:33.137
even in a game with strangers they would
never meet.

00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:37.693
In a later study, it still stung when
people were told the other players

00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:38.908
were just a computer.

00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:43.835
And in the same research, people who had
been left out were more likely to

00:02:43.855 --> 00:02:45.900
go along with the group afterwards.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.576
Exclusion makes us try harder to fit back
in.

00:02:49.546 --> 00:02:52.838
At work, being ignored is often treated as
harmless,

00:02:52.858 --> 00:02:54.768
nothing like being shouted at.

00:02:55.248 --> 00:02:58.992
But one study found it was more strongly
linked to feeling you don't

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.946
belong, and to people leaving their jobs
years later.

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:04.845
Then there's the word itself.

00:03:05.225 --> 00:03:10.187
In one study, people sending emails
expected readers to catch their tone,

00:03:10.207 --> 00:03:14.439
serious or sarcastic, about seventy-eight
percent of the time.

00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:18.666
The readers got it right about fifty-six
percent of the time.

00:03:18.686 --> 00:03:20.601
Barely better than a coin flip.

00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:24.497
Without a voice or a face, the reader
fills in the tone.

00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:27.197
And when we're worried, we fill it in
darker.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:29.479
And that empty chair?

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:32.860
Probably fewer people noticed it than
Daniel thought.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.630
Students made to wear an embarrassing
T-shirt into a room guessed that

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.714
about half the people there noticed. About
a quarter actually did.

00:03:42.294 --> 00:03:44.659
We are the main character in our own
heads.

00:03:44.679 --> 00:03:47.841
Everyone else is busy being the main
character in theirs.

00:03:49.011 --> 00:03:53.583
None of this proved Daniel wrong. Maybe he
really was being frozen out.

00:03:54.163 --> 00:03:56.698
But the facts were much thinner than the
story,

00:03:56.718 --> 00:03:58.675
and the story was keeping him awake.

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:02.288
There was only one way to find out which
one was true.

00:04:03.938 --> 00:04:08.347
On Tuesday morning, Daniel had two
options. Keep polishing the story,

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:09.523
or ask a question.

00:04:10.103 --> 00:04:13.016
He started with the person closest to the
facts.

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:17.388
<v Daniel>Can I ask you something, without it being
weird?

00:04:17.408 --> 00:04:20.294
<v Daniel>Was I taken off the Ostrel call on
purpose?

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:24.744
<v Leah>Graham set the list. He said you'd want a
break from Ostrel,

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:25.775
<v Leah>after everything.

00:04:26.475 --> 00:04:28.486
<v Leah>I assumed you'd asked for it.

00:04:29.486 --> 00:04:30.486
<v Daniel>I didn't.

00:04:31.355 --> 00:04:34.128
<v Leah>Then ask to come back. I want you in that
room.

00:04:35.618 --> 00:04:38.219
<v Leah>For what it's worth, I noticed you weren't
there.

00:04:38.239 --> 00:04:39.844
<v Leah>I don't think anyone else did.

00:04:42.414 --> 00:04:44.121
Then he wrote to Graham.

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:47.720
A fact. A request. And an open door,

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.352
in case there was a reason he didn't know.

00:04:51.132 --> 00:04:53.340
No accusation anywhere in it.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.080
<v Graham>Daniel. I took you off to lighten the
load.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.927
<v Graham>I thought you'd earned a breather.

00:05:00.827 --> 00:05:04.647
<v Daniel>I appreciate that. I'd rather decide my
own load.

00:05:05.847 --> 00:05:08.578
<v Graham>Fair enough. I'll add you back.

00:05:10.068 --> 00:05:14.566
Was it kindness? Or a quiet way of moving
Daniel to the edge of the room?

00:05:14.586 --> 00:05:16.319
Graham's answer fitted both.

00:05:16.999 --> 00:05:19.139
And Daniel didn't need to know which.

00:05:19.159 --> 00:05:21.378
He only needed to be back in the room.

00:05:22.828 --> 00:05:27.113
Here's the part most of us get wrong. In a
series of studies, people

00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:31.735
asking strangers for help expected to need
about twice as many asks as it

00:05:31.755 --> 00:05:32.755
actually took.

00:05:33.221 --> 00:05:36.300
We focus on what helping costs the other
person,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.645
and forget how awkward it is to say no to
a direct, polite request.

00:05:41.815 --> 00:05:44.958
<v Daniel>Ruth, could I have fifteen minutes with
you once a month?

00:05:44.978 --> 00:05:47.142
<v Daniel>I'd like to know what you need from me.

00:05:48.142 --> 00:05:50.586
<v Ruth>Of course. I was wondering when you'd ask.

00:05:51.386 --> 00:05:55.778
<v Ruth>And if something changes and you don't
know why, come and ask me first.

00:05:57.748 --> 00:06:01.207
<v Leah>Good, everyone's here. Daniel, start us
off with Carlton?

00:06:02.007 --> 00:06:06.610
<v Daniel>Happy to. Carlton's evening runs look a
lot like Northfield's did.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.237
That afternoon, a new invite arrived.
Carlton planning. Graham,

00:06:12.257 --> 00:06:13.257
and Daniel.

00:06:13.663 --> 00:06:17.707
The whole evening-runs plan started with a
pattern Leah spotted.

00:06:17.727 --> 00:06:19.124
Her name wasn't on it.

00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:31.204
<v Graham>Leah. Good. I was just about to add you.

00:06:33.574 --> 00:06:36.433
Maybe Graham forgot Leah. Maybe he didn't.

00:06:37.213 --> 00:06:39.848
Exclusion rarely looks like a slammed
door.

00:06:40.628 --> 00:06:43.463
It looks like a door that nobody holds
open.

00:06:46.113 --> 00:06:49.300
So if you're ever quietly left out of
something at work,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.465
here are four moves.

00:06:50.845 --> 00:06:54.344
First, separate what happened from the
story you're telling yourself.

00:06:54.524 --> 00:06:58.769
Ask the person closest to the facts,
before you decide what it means.

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:03.576
Ask for what you want, in writing. A fact,
a request, and an open door.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.733
And when you notice someone else missing
from the list,

00:07:06.753 --> 00:07:07.788
forward the invite.

00:07:10.038 --> 00:07:13.174
Have you ever been quietly left out of
something at work?

00:07:13.954 --> 00:07:15.336
Did you ask why?
